<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Petar I Petrović-Njegoš</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1748">1748</a>–<a href="/years/1830">1830</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Saint Peter of Cetinje</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Petar I Petrović-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petar-ii-petrovic-njegos">Njegoš</a> was prince-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of Montenegro from 1782 to 1830. He defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kara-mahmud-pasha-bushati">Kara Mahmud Pasha Bushati</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shkoder">Scutari</a> at Martinići and Krusi in 1796, securing Montenegro's de facto independence, and issued its first law code. He is venerated as a saint, Saint Peter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cetinje">Cetinje</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/petar-i-petrovic-njegos.jpg" alt="Petar I Petrović-Njegoš" width="320" height="416" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Andra Gavrilović · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Petar_I_Petrovi%C4%87-Njego%C5%A1_%D0%97%D0%BD%D0%B0%D0%BC%D0%B5%D0%BD%D0%B8%D1%82%D0%B8_%D0%A1%D1%80%D0%B1%D0%B8_XIX._%D0%B2%D0%B5%D0%BA%D0%B0.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
